questions.question:
A hydrogen atom emits photons due to electron transitions. The transition from an unknown energy level $n$ to the level $n=2$ emits a photon of frequency $
u_1$. The transition from the level $n=3$ to the level $n=2$ emits a photon of frequency $
u_2$. If the ratio $\dfrac{
u_1}{
u_2} = \dfrac{27}{20}$, the value of the principal quantum number $n$ is

questions.solution:
The frequency of a photon emitted during an electron transition in a hydrogen atom is given by Rydberg's formula:<br>$
u = cR \left( \dfrac{1}{n_f^2} - \dfrac{1}{n_i^2} ight)$<br><br>For the first transition ($n 	o 2$):<br>$
u_1 = cR \left( \dfrac{1}{2^2} - \dfrac{1}{n^2} ight) = cR \left( \dfrac{1}{4} - \dfrac{1}{n^2} ight)$<br><br>For the second transition ($3 	o 2$):<br>$
u_2 = cR \left( \dfrac{1}{2^2} - \dfrac{1}{3^2} ight) = cR \left( \dfrac{1}{4} - \dfrac{1}{9} ight) = cR \left( \dfrac{5}{36} ight)$<br><br>Given the ratio $\dfrac{
u_1}{
u_2} = \dfrac{27}{20}$, we have:<br>$\dfrac{\dfrac{1}{4} - \dfrac{1}{n^2}}{\dfrac{5}{36}} = \dfrac{27}{20}$<br><br>$\dfrac{1}{4} - \dfrac{1}{n^2} = \dfrac{27}{20} 	imes \dfrac{5}{36}$<br><br>$\dfrac{1}{4} - \dfrac{1}{n^2} = \dfrac{3 	imes 9 	imes 5}{4 	imes 5 	imes 4 	imes 9} = \dfrac{3}{16}$<br><br>$\dfrac{1}{n^2} = \dfrac{1}{4} - \dfrac{3}{16} = \dfrac{4}{16} - \dfrac{3}{16} = \dfrac{1}{16}$<br><br>$n^2 = 16 \implies n = 4$<br><br>Answer: $4$

